        "body": "Use consecutive work sample cases where possible, then stratify by owner, channel, and risk class. If a source cannot be accessed, mark it unavailable and record the access problem rather than asking the operator to reconstruct it from memory. A sample of recent closures can miss the oldest unresolved work, which is often the most informative. Record the sampling frame as an export with a date, filter, and row count. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported 30-day retention. The October 28 work sample note should distinguish a missing work sample from a confirmed negative.\n\nStop expanding the sample only when the review question is answered, not when the result starts to look favorable. If a source cannot be accessed, mark it unavailable and record the access problem rather than asking the operator to reconstruct it from memory. Record the eligible count and every exclusion before the sample is drawn. Record the date of the sample and the version of the queue export. Link the observation to the exact field in the interview scheduler and to the interview notes that supports it. The HR lead decision on work sample should be dated October 28 and linked to the interview notes.\n\nUse consecutive work sample cases where possible, then stratify by owner, channel, and risk class. Record the time of each observation so a later reviewer can distinguish a late entry from a changed decision. Keep the sample selection reproducible by recording the export date, filter, and random seed or start rule. Use a random start and a fixed interval when the frame is ordered, and record both. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time. The October 28 work sample close should name the destination field, the approver, and the time to slate check."
